Network IDS Integration: Suricata + Wazuh
Overview

The lab originally had one layer of detection: a Wazuh agent installed directly on DC1, watching that host's own logs and events. That's host-based detection — it only sees what happens on the machine.

This update adds a second, different kind of detection: a network-based IDS watching the traffic between machines, not just what happens on one of them. Together, the two give the lab a real "defense in depth" setup:

Host-based (Wazuh agent on DC1): catches things that happen locally — failed logins, file changes, process activity.
Network-based (Suricata): catches things that happen on the wire — a port scan, unusual traffic patterns, connections to ports that shouldn't be talked to.

Both now report into the same place: the Wazuh dashboard.

Why Suricata instead of Snort

The original plan was to use Snort, since it's the more commonly-referenced tool in study material. In practice, Snort isn't currently packaged for Ubuntu 26.04 ("Resolute") — the OS this lab's Wazuh-Manager VM runs. Rather than fight an unsupported package, I switched to Suricata, which is:

Actively maintained and fully packaged for this OS version
Multi-threaded (faster on modern hardware than Snort's older architecture)
Increasingly the more common choice in real SOC environments today

Lesson: sometimes the "textbook" tool isn't the right practical choice, and knowing when to substitute a better-supported equivalent is itself a useful skill.

