Setup Summary
Installed Suricata on the Wazuh-Manager VM (sudo apt install suricata), after enabling the universe repository.
Pointed Suricata at the correct network interface (enp0s8, the one facing intnet — not the NAT adapter).
Pulled the free Emerging Threats Open ruleset with suricata-update (~52,700 signatures).
Configured Wazuh to read Suricata's structured alert log (/var/log/suricata/eve.json) by adding a <localfile> block to ossec.conf and restarting the Wazuh manager service.
Verified the full pipeline by running an Nmap SYN scan from Kali-Attacker against DC1 and confirming the alert appeared both in Suricata's own log and in the Wazuh dashboard.
Troubleshooting Journey

This integration didn't work on the first try — or the fifth. Documenting the actual debugging path, because the process is more instructive than the final config:

Symptom	Root Cause	Fix
apt install snort failed with "Unable to locate package"	Snort isn't packaged for Ubuntu 26.04 (Resolute) at all	Switched to Suricata, which is
Suricata ran, but the alert log stayed empty even with real traffic confirmed reaching DC1	Suricata was watching from a third host (Wazuh-Manager) on a VirtualBox internal network — by default it only sees traffic addressed to itself, not traffic between two other VMs	Enabled Promiscuous Mode → Allow All on the Wazuh-Manager VM's intnet adapter in VirtualBox
Still no alerts, and an af-packet: cluster-id 99 already in use error appeared	A systemd-managed Suricata service had auto-started in the background and was fighting my manual foreground test runs for the same network socket	Stopped and disabled the systemd service (systemctl stop/disable suricata) so only one instance runs at a time
A custom "match anything" test rule still didn't fire	My rule lived in local.rules, but that file was never actually referenced in suricata.yaml's rule-files: list — Suricata never read it	Added /etc/suricata/rules/local.rules to the rule-files: list
Even the full 52,700-rule Emerging Threats ruleset never matched the Nmap scan	Most ET signatures are written to detect $EXTERNAL_NET -> $HOME_NET traffic (an outside attacker). Since Kali-Attacker is inside the same /24 as everything else, this was internal-to-internal traffic, which most rules explicitly ignore	Wrote a custom rule scoped to $HOME_NET -> $HOME_NET, matching Nmap's known SYN-scan fingerprint (TCP window size 1024)

The real lesson from this section: an IDS that "starts successfully" and prints no errors is not the same as an IDS that is actually working. Every layer — capture visibility, process conflicts, rule loading, and rule logic — had to be verified independently with raw tools (tcpdump, ps aux, suricata -T -v) before trusting the end-to-end result.

The Detection Rule

Instead of relying only on the generic Emerging Threats ruleset, I added a rule specific to this lab, matching the exact fingerprint of the Nmap scan traffic observed via tcpdump:

alert tcp $HOME_NET any -> $HOME_NET any (msg:"LOCAL SCAN Possible Nmap -sS scan (window 1024)"; flags:S; window:1024; classtype:attempted-recon; sid:9000002; rev:1;)

This mirrors a real Emerging Threats signature (ET SCAN NMAP -sS window 1024, sid 2009582), adapted so it also fires on internal-to-internal traffic — appropriate for a lab where the "attacker" and "victim" live on the same segment.

Result
Suricata detects the Nmap SYN scan from Kali-Attacker in real time, both in its own log and in the Wazuh dashboard.
Detection now happens at two independent layers (network + host), correlated in one place.
The lab now demonstrates not just "I installed a tool," but a realistic detection engineering workflow: deploy, test, debug, and document.
